  "extraction_notes": [
    {
      "note_type": "inconsistency",
      "note": "The abstract states 'family-wise error corrected with a minimal cluster size of 20 voxels' but Tables 2 and 3 and the results section state 'family-wise error corrected with a minimal cluster size of 30 voxels'. Both thresholds were preserved as reported."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "inconsistency",
      "note": "Table 2 reports the left Middle Temporal Gyrus with X coordinate = 56 (positive), yet the hemisphere is labeled 'L'. In Talairach space, positive X typically indicates right hemisphere. This may be a typographical error in the paper. The value was transcribed exactly as reported."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "inconsistency",
      "note": "Table 2 reports the right frontal lobe decrease with X coordinate = -8 (negative), yet the hemisphere is labeled 'R'. In Talairach space, negative X typically indicates left hemisphere. Table 3 similarly reports right inferior frontal gyrus with X = -18 (negative) yet labeled 'R'. These may be typographical errors. Values transcribed exactly as reported."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "ambiguity",
      "note": "The right middle cingulate cortex is mentioned as a GMV decrease region in Group A in the abstract and results text but does not appear as a separate row in Table 2. A narrative-only contrast entry was created to capture this discrepancy."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "ambiguity",
      "note": "The coordinate space is labeled 'Talairach' in the table headers, but the preprocessing pipeline normalizes to MNI space. It is possible the coordinates were converted from MNI to Talairach for reporting, or that the label reflects the table column header convention only. No explicit conversion statement was made in the paper."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "No sham acupuncture arm was included in this trial. There is no control for needle placement or placebo needle effect. The comparison is acupuncture plus conventional vs. conventional alone."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "Deqi was described as intended and as a procedural goal but was not formally measured with any validated instrument (e.g., MASS, MGH Acupuncture Sensation Scale). No quantitative deqi data were reported."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "Expectancy, credibility, and blinding adequacy were not formally assessed or reported beyond the statement that group assignment was unknown for patients."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "Adverse events were not reported. No statement was made about whether adverse events occurred or were systematically collected."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "No follow-up timepoint beyond post-treatment (4 weeks) was assessed."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "The paper reports the lesion locations were 'primarily in the left basal ganglia' but does not provide individual lesion maps or confirm whether lesion location was a covariate in the VBM analysis."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "The flip angle value is reported as '9°' in the methods section, which is unusually small for a spin-echo planar sequence and may be a typographical error in the original paper. Transcribed exactly as reported."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "Field of view is reported as '16 × 16 mm2' which appears to be an OCR or typographical error in the original (likely 160 × 160 mm or 256 × 256 mm). Transcribed exactly as reported."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "missing_information",
      "note": "The number of slices (176) and voxel size (1 × 1 × 1 mm3) suggest a T1-weighted structural sequence, not a functional sequence. The paper labels it 'VBM protocol' using a 'spin-echo planar image sequence', which is unusual for VBM T1-weighted images. This may reflect an OCR artifact or unusual acquisition labeling. The neuroimaging modality was classified as rs-fMRI per the paper's abstract and methods referencing resting-state fMRI scans, while the VBM analysis was performed on T1-weighted structural images."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "ambiguity",
      "note": "The paper states all patients accepted 'resting-state functional magnetic resonance (fMRI) scan' and VBM analysis was performed. VBM typically uses T1-weighted structural MRI, not resting-state fMRI BOLD sequences. The acquisition parameters described (TR=1900ms, TE=2.26ms, 1×1×1mm voxel, 176 slices) are consistent with a T1-weighted structural scan. The paper appears to use the term 'fMRI' loosely to describe the MRI session, with actual VBM analysis performed on T1 structural images."
    },
    {
      "note_type": "other",
      "note": "Sample size is small (n=21 total; 11 in Group A, 10 in Group B). Authors acknowledge this in the Limitations section."
    }
  ]
